WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
At the time I was at Mossville, that was my first teaching experience.

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:13.920
I enjoyed going to school every day because the parents, they was real, real knowledgeable, they was concerned about their children.

3
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:20.980
Later in life, I could see they must . . . Mossville must have been built up and they had good teachers and all,

4
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:27.360
because for one, the parents was very knowledgeable, and you know, they looked like they had gone to school and be there.

5
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:34.380
And the community still . . . It looked like it was still kind of like a closed community, you know.

6
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.800
It wasn’t integrated there before.

7
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.560
They were all black back there, you know.

8
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:50.800
But when they closed Mossville, they was closing down when . . . law it was changing about integration, they sent me to Westwood [Elementary].

9
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:58.840
That’s when the blacks had to go to the white school and the white came to the black school,

10
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.520
and the white went to the white . . . the black went to the white school.

11
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.860
And they sent me to Westwood.

12
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.940
And I’m telling you,

13
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:14.320
when I went to Westwood that was the most . . . I would hate to get up to go to work the next morning,

14
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.040
because the way they treat . . . they didn’t respect you.

15
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:26.740
When I went to Westwood, I was doing the same thing with these children, hugging them like I did my little black children over there.

16
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.940
And the day come . . . Oh, a little girl came to me.

17
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.080
Every time I think about it, I just feel so sad.

18
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.040
I wonder if that child is still living somewhere.

19
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:42.400
Her daddy . . . I think she came, and the next morning she said, “My daddy said, ‘Don’t you put your nigger hands on me.’”

20
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.620
And here I’m crying, running with tears in my eyes to Mr. Burns.

21
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.280
He was nice, though.

22
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.430
But he was the principal.

23
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.600
He say, “Mrs. Guidry, I’m sorry.

24
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.820
Don’t feel like that.”

25
00:01:51.820 --> 00:02:00.180
I said, “Just the idea, this child going to tell me her daddy say, ‘Don’t put his . . .her nigger hands on her.’

26
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:00.840
And that was it.”
